  "overview": {
    "asOf": "2026-10-14",
    "items": [
      {
        "text": "Robert G. Luna is the 34th Sheriff of Los Angeles County, first elected in 2022. He previously served as Long Beach Police Chief, ending a 36-year career at that department. As chief during the 2020 George Floyd protests, he drew criticism from small business owners, local officials, and some department officers over his response. He leads the largest sheriff's department in the United States, with nearly 18,000 budgeted staff.",
        "cites": [1, 2, 3],
        "sentences": [
          {
            "text": "Robert G. Luna is the 34th Sheriff of Los Angeles County, first elected in 2022.",
            "cites": [1]
          },
          {
            "text": "He previously served as Long Beach Police Chief, ending a 36-year career at that department.",
            "cites": [1]
          },
          {
            "text": "As chief during the 2020 George Floyd protests, he drew criticism from small business owners, local officials, and some department officers over his response.",
            "cites": [2]
          },
          {
            "text": "He leads the largest sheriff's department in the United States, with nearly 18,000 budgeted staff.",
            "cites": [3]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "His campaign says the department reported a 14% decline in homicides and increased crime clearance rates during his first term. His campaign also says he established the Office of Constitutional Policing and banned deputy gangs. A county oversight body documented a 16% reduction in custody use of force and an 11% reduction in patrol use of force. It also noted a 42% drop in shooting-involved deaths. He is planning a Correctional Care Center for inmates, over half of whom have mental health or substance use issues. The department is replacing its decades-old computer dispatch system and deploying drones and license plate readers.",
        "cites": [4, 5]
      },
      {
        "text": "His campaign says he will reduce violent crime, build public trust, modernize department technology, and address homelessness. He faces staffing shortages of about 1,400 sworn and 1,300 professional vacancies and the need to secure large-scale events such as the 2026 World Cup. In 2025, he requested National Guard deployment and imposed curfews to stop looting during wildfires, leading to 20 arrests. In September 2026, the U.S. Department of Justice opened an investigation into the physical conditions at Men's Central Jail.",
        "cites": [6, 5, 7, 8],
        "sentences": [
          {
            "text": "His campaign says he will reduce violent crime, build public trust, modernize department technology, and address homelessness.",
            "cites": [6]
          },
          {
            "text": "He faces staffing shortages of about 1,400 sworn and 1,300 professional vacancies and the need to secure large-scale events such as the 2026 World Cup.",
            "cites": [5]
          },
          {
            "text": "In 2025, he requested National Guard deployment and imposed curfews to stop looting during wildfires, leading to 20 arrests.",
            "cites": [7]
          },
          {
            "text": "In September 2026, the U.S. Department of Justice opened an investigation into the physical conditions at Men's Central Jail.",
            "cites": [8]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
